  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nA weak frontal boundary will push into the area tonight and wash\nout by midday Wednesday. The model blend produces 15-20% \nchances of showers and thunderstorms across all but far \nsoutheast counties between midnight tonight through 9am \nWednesday morning. South winds will increase Wednesday morning to\n10-20mph, with afternoon gusts of 25-30mph.\n\nBetter chances of showers and storms arrive Wednesday evening\nand overnight with the approach of another cold front. The front\nessentially stays just north of the area, and keeps the best\nchances (50-70%) across the northern counties, with chances\ndecreasing to 15-30s as the spread southeast across the rest of\nthe forecast area late Wednesday night into Thursday.  \n\nSeveral disturbances/boundaries will continue to move near or\ninto the forecast area Friday, through the weekend and even into\nnext week. The model blend produces good chances (35-70%) of \nshowers and thunderstorms Thursday night through Saturday \nevening, with similar chances returning late Sunday night\nthrough Tuesday. Current timing shows a bit of a lull late\nSaturday night through Sunday evening with only 15-25% chances,\nbut timing tweaks are still possible based on the movement of\nthe disturbances. Severe storms  \n\nWell above normal temperatures will continue for the next week.\nThe warmest period will be tonight through Friday with lows in\nthe 50s to around 60 degrees, and highs in the middle 70s to\naround 80 degrees. Clouds, precipitation, and eventually\nnortheast to easterly winds, will brings highs down into the\nmiddle 60s to around 70 degrees, with lows remaining in the \n50s. Even more of a cooling trend will begin Tuesday.",
